TERMIC NATIVE AMIGA

Native Workbench client and local C development environment for AmigaOS 3.x.
Requires 68020+, GadTools, Roadshow (or compatible bsdsocket.library), AmiSSL
5.23 or a compatible newer installation, and a correctly set system clock.
This is a first native client, with one C project. It is not the Tauri app.

INSTALL
Unpack the archive into a new drawer. In an Amiga Shell, assign the drawer:
  Assign Termic: Work:TermicNative
  Execute Termic:InstallSDK
  Execute Termic:BuildNative
Replace Work:TermicNative with the location you chose.
Create the Workbench icon if needed:
  Termic:TermicNative --icon Termic:TermicNative
Then double-click TermicNative. It assigns Termic: to its own drawer.
No Mac, Docker, bridge, or LAN model server is required at runtime.

USE
Source: edit Project/main.c in Ed. Save and close Ed before building.
Build: compile locally with VBCC. Output: Project/Program and build.log.
Run: launch the compiled program. Only this button runs generated code.
Task: edit Project/task.txt. The sample asks for a counter change.
API key: enter your OpenAI API key as one line in Config/api-key.
Model: choose an API model your account can use. Default: gpt-5-mini.
HTTPS test: verified connection to OpenAI, expecting HTTP 401 without a key.
Agent: send the task to the cloud; allow reading/writing main.c and builds.
Log: show the transcript of agent responses, tools and compiler diagnostics.
Ed: press Escape, type X, then Return to save and exit.

The key stays in the local Config/api-key file. Do not put it in chat,
screenshots, Git, project source or backups shared with others. Classic
AmigaOS has no per-user file isolation. This file is not encrypted.
The agent sends the task, main.c and compiler results to OpenAI. Responses
are stored by the API to continue the tool conversation. It makes at most
eight requests per run. API access and usage billing must be enabled.
The key is sent only as the HTTPS Authorization header to api.openai.com.
There is no plaintext fallback or disabled certificate verification.

The agent keeps the prior edit in Project/main.c.bak. Review source before
Run. Compiler jobs and generated programs have normal AmigaOS access;
the fixed tool list is not an operating-system sandbox. The client does
not expose a shell-command tool to the model.

LIMITS
One main.c file, at most 32 KiB; task at most 8 KiB; tool build log 16 KiB;
HTTP response 256 KiB; each network exchange 120 seconds after DNS lookup.
DNS uses the networking stack's resolver timeout. Ctrl-C cancels socket
waits; the worker also checks between model steps. No streaming, Git,
parallel agents or multi-file projects in this first client.
The window remains responsive while a separate DOS process runs the job.
If a worker crashes, remove Project/.busy only after checking it stopped.
Do not run two installations at once: the Termic: assign selects one root.

ZZ9000
Uses the existing Workbench/RTG screen. No ARM payload is launched and no
firmware, core ownership, DDR address, cache or display mode is changed.
Compilation, file tools and HTTPS currently run on the 68k. ARM acceleration
needs a separate measured compute workload and verified core reservation.

